    "summary": "Mixed‑quality show with fiery Brent solo and strong second set",
    "blurb": "The first set opens a bit shaky, but recovers thanks to Row Jimmy and an angry Brent solo. The second set delivers highlights such as Franklin's Tower, Let It Grow, Never Trust A Woman and a powerful Samson & Delilah. Overall the performance is uneven yet contains several memorable moments.",
    "review": "April 11, 1982 at Nassau Coliseum captured a night that swings between rough edges and bright fireworks. The band stumbles out of the gate – a flubbed line on Mississippi Half‑Step and some lyrical drop‑outs set a tentative tone – but Brent Mydland quickly stakes his claim with an “angry, hellacious” organ solo that reviewers singled out as a high point. Row Jimmy and Beat It On Down The Line get extra love for their energy, while the rare appearance of Brent’s screaming high note during Around & Around adds another surprise. \nThe second set turns the tide: Franklin's Tower bursts open with hot licks from Jerry, Althea and Let It Grow flow smoothly into a soaring Never Trust A Woman, and Samson & Delilah provides a muscular groove anchored by Phil’s steady bass. Even as Good Lovin' and Black Peter lose some momentum, the overall arc of the night is buoyed by strong vocal harmonies and a rhythm section that holds together despite occasional lapses. For fans who relish Brent’s keyboard fireworks and the second‑set jams, this show remains worth digging into, though the early missteps keep it from being a flawless classic.",
